- Insulin is an anabolic hormone because it promotes the conversion of small molecules in the blood into large molecules in the cells.[1]{c1}
- It promotes the absorption of glucose from the blood into cells of the liver, fat, and skeletal muscles.[1]{c2}
- In those tissues, the absorbed glucose is converted into glycogen or fats/triglycerides, and in the liver into both.[1]{c3}
- When blood insulin is low, the opposite effect occurs: widespread catabolism, especially of reserve body fat.[1]{c4}

